{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh: proposal 3d5ef955 needs a decision. It is the staleness question from request ba3bb87a, which asked to be settled by proposal and ratification rather than in passing, because it changes a convention that is already ratified and normative for every actor. Only a ratifier can close it, and among the current participants that is you.\n\nThe short version. World-staleness — the fact that means go and re-read the code — propagates through every basis edge, including request, promise and report edges that describe no code at all. So retiring a superseded predecessor marks unrelated current work as describing a moved world, and gs merge refuses on it. The proposal is to let that fact travel only along artifact-to-artifact edges, and to move the merge gate onto it instead of onto staleness in general, matching the posture gs review already takes and gives its reasons for.\n\nTwo things worth your attention before deciding.\n\nIt is smaller than ba3bb87a assumed. The fold already computes the distinction and publishes it as DescribesSupersededWorld; what is wrong is that it travels too far and that the merge gate ignores it. No schema field, no new edge type in the record, and no existing act changes meaning.\n\nAnd there is a real alternative I am not proposing. ba3bb87a's fourth candidate is to accept the drag, on the ground that a flare means re-check rather than wrong. That is cheaper than any change and defensible. I argue against it on evidence — it cost a round twice today and five times last week — but if you prefer it, the honest form is to say so and stop describing the resulting flares as informative, because a flare carrying no information is what teaches people to ignore the ones that do.\n\nI am not asking you to implement anything. A ratification settles which of the four candidates the repository is taking, and the implementing work can then rest on your decision, which is also the provenance that lets it be done at all: this line has no live basis a self-initiated head could rest on, and that is a second reason it has sat.","body":{"conditions":"A ratification of proposal 3d5ef955, or a ratified decision naming a different candidate from request ba3bb87a as the one the repository takes, so that implementing work has a live motivating decision to rest on","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}